 ## Background
-Neural Illumination Fields (NeIF) was proposed to address the limitations of previous illumination representations. Traditional methods like environment maps and Spherical Gaussians assume illumination is infinitely far away, failing to handle environment occlusion and directional lighting practically [S1]. Follow-up methods like NeILF modeled incident illumination maps for each surface point but ignored spatial distance constraints, leading to material-lighting ambiguity in complex 3D environments [S1]. NeIF builds on the success of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and their ability to recover detailed geometry and material, extending this to a fully differentiable framework for 3D ambient illumination [S1]. It was demonstrated as a plug-and-play module for Mip-NeRF, allowing objects to have a better sense of belonging in new 3D environments [S1].
+Neural Illumination Fields (NeIF) was proposed to address the limitations of previous illumination representations [S1]. Traditional methods like environment maps and Spherical Gaussians assume illumination is infinitely far away, failing to handle environment occlusion and directional lighting practically [S1]. Follow-up methods like NeILF modeled incident illumination maps for each surface point but ignored spatial distance constraints, leading to material-lighting ambiguity in complex 3D environments [S1]. NeIF builds on the success of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and their ability to recover detailed geometry and material, extending this to a fully differentiable framework for 3D ambient illumination [S1]. It was demonstrated as a plug-and-play module for Mip-NeRF, allowing objects to have a better sense of belonging in new 3D environments [S1].
 
